{:edb/db-before-t 1513 :edb/db-after-t 1514 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534826 50 #inst "2026-10-12T16:50:30.527Z" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186128838 1071 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/40/40212b3cc1bb25e4d42b96ba9fd2c1958772a32fe69136dca347be295956b9ea.png)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/de/de70592e2668d2ef6030843fe9e20dd3da9fc1edec2501e7cd318a57682f3f0e.png)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186128838 1071 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/7f/7fc9eb28594744f04c1934d10b7e9751c9f9855897f4cc4da05b8a5e82a93f3d.svg)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/5e/5e056d63a91b0f4ae7dcb1c076764b862b0321b47c6e586cc91acd9e01dd36c8.svg)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186128838 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/37/370aba8042cc3dbd6c5c9dc15751e50dd8fe7be36d0cb220966da075bf9e2e43.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/1c/1ca433e68fd2a57e9bd9666b76c0577fd84621eededde3a50d03a49723b21739.svg) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186128838 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/9b/9b0ee6271962e3ede406351f05d805ae60f4b37ce5a45489b117ab3786ed3c23.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/72/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186128860 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}.$ Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](images/4c/4c41597b6c647ba16bff7b6675d1bbe9d3c66b8cbb8e9a13bbb6651da6cd284e.png)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186128860 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}.$ Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](images/ff/ff668919204d7ccd57c1711fed961ca73562d47cc6f100511b06fe54a5ea212d.svg)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186128861 1071 "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](images/4c/4cb994a50c42c48bad095fb056406cdcebd42143b3c0ce041ad06f52a2942a01.png)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186128861 1071 "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](images/57/57874e6e685ee9fb1a2b527fe046696aa983b04d22751c72d8a76783b349ca18.svg)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186128861 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n![](images/45/4595adcbeb5ee3dc0bbe1258536e9b0e3e2fc81d5720a3394029df814f55f949.svg)\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/2b/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186128861 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n![](images/e9/e947cb962ec1c0405292be18c838205b7be95ab083e07925d797b87e9c5b8654.png)\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/2b/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186129006 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/ce/ce4692599c5c9fe69ed9247d65dee5d982e1b7a2cf33b9070b799c94c321bad4.png)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186129006 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/eb/eb8cb887e2c6e273a9a59bdbffeb0e8a2af4f8dcf5cc376ba4318128201ef936.svg)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186129006 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/59/59265be9c026880542032006b0c4aec9f1c8eeb2cd448423d38c17f75e1393cd.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186129006 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/92/928c9f7761e597884525a92388346c486c4e0df9c2c942cf31ad87164676613e.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186129187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437214 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186129344 1050 900.0 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186129509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126554 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186129525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126649 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186130392 1064 "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nRecall that, under this notation,\n\n- $\\mathbf u = [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n- $\\mathbf x = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that\n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186130392 1064 "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions. \n\nLet  $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write\n![](images/11/1137221ffc72c194aba941eaee899cc0a4134a92e92e86cfa65c88acd5f702ec.svg) \n\nRecall that, under this notation, \n\n- ![](images/0d/0decb326ae66f97fb75a8df874479061fb1557b0437ca8ea1e7c5ed645db4b19.svg) represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n\n- ![](images/09/091b7a3ddf6ad7dc35360e728c2946481b3a17f60525d09676eb469ebd518a4d.svg) represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n![](images/23/239781240f2def3a5adf5331f36fa6dbdd3657ab431195c30cf3ede49d2ef3e2.svg) \n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that \n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186130460 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n![](images/f9/f9a1c3375eed570284c4e001f119f21901f4493a0b851919b92b678a74187444.svg) \n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that  $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$ \n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n![](images/b4/b433bcafea1c84c67f2fb65ac8343a4d8401ce6d4e745f6d464b010a4258164a.svg) \n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =u{cos}^{2}⁡v+u{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186130460 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$\n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}v & -u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v & u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ & =u{cos}^{2}v+u{sin}^{2}v \\\\ & =u({cos}^{2}v+{sin}^{2}v) \\\\ & =u.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186130461 1064 "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation\n\n$$\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186130461 1064 "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186130462 1064 "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones.\n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$J(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson).\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$.\n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)< 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" 13194139534826 17592186045417 false] [17592186130462 1064 "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones. \n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where\n![](images/d0/d0149013f12e5cdad31611d4c64489c0aaf6049707e9eed22202ae70d2d8a38a.svg) \n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$\nJ(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson). \n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$. \n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)<  0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437208 1080 "2{u}^{2}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-1)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437208 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437208 1083 #uuid "3ee2cdb4-9a34-5a8b-a223-2ea4a089aa37" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437209 1080 "4uv(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-1)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437209 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437209 1083 #uuid "4c763f52-9b23-5041-b90c-5411e27f7574" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437210 1080 "2{u}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-v)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437210 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437210 1083 #uuid "962f026a-a6e1-5e0c-8e81-b3f195d58e6c" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437211 1080 "2uv(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-v)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437211 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437211 1083 #uuid "97d54cc4-bb7e-5809-af16-75ed6fdc3a2d" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437212 1080 "4{u}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-v)" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437212 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437212 1083 #uuid "fc2ceb4d-c670-5d4a-ba41-be274f566d61" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437208 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437209 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437210 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437211 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437212 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1077 "selection" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1078 #edb/ref 17592186437208 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437213 1079 #uuid "07041bd7-da85-5245-8203-4f70786b0ce5" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437213 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437214 1069 "q-81263" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437214 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v \\\\ {u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437214 1072 #uuid "fdaff75b-e0c6-5e92-b720-ea0f3e9d5336" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true] [17592186437214 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x=u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v$ and $y={u}^{2}{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{u}{v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2{u}^{2}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v & \\frac{u}{v} \\\\ 2u{v}^{2} & 2{u}^{2}v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot 2{u}^{2}v-\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot 2u{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534826 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-3ee2cdb4-9a34-5a8b-a223-2ea4a089aa37" 17592186437208 "answer-4c763f52-9b23-5041-b90c-5411e27f7574" 17592186437209 "answer-962f026a-a6e1-5e0c-8e81-b3f195d58e6c" 17592186437210 "answer-97d54cc4-bb7e-5809-af16-75ed6fdc3a2d" 17592186437211 "answer-fc2ceb4d-c670-5d4a-ba41-be274f566d61" 17592186437212 "answer-field-07041bd7-da85-5245-8203-4f70786b0ce5" 17592186437213 "question-fdaff75b-e0c6-5e92-b720-ea0f3e9d5336" 17592186437214} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
